en really  focused on showing real women we  realized that we had fallen short and  for us it was so important to to fill  that you know that space and to  celebrate plus-sized women and do better  by the plus-size community what's the  response been from readers that's far  you know we get so many really moving  letters from women saying thank you I've  never seen myself represented in in  media before I grew up not seeing  someone that looked like me and just  really a lot of appreciation for being  being seen and being recognized Denise  can you see it happening in front of  your eyes today the changing perception  absolutely I mean I started modeling  about ten years ago and what I find so  remarkable is that I've been able to not  only be a driving force behind this  change that we're now starting to see  but also to be able to mold the next  generation because now what they're zine  is so different from what we grew up  with we're creating empowering strong  opinionated and confident women little  girls walking around so I can't wait to  see what that does to them what did it  mean to you I love how you got me so  they say you made history walking in  this straight sighs what did it mean to  you to do that number one what did that  feel like n you wrote a very moving  essay in refinery  twenty-nine about your own mother  struggle you wanted to tell that story  but first what it meant for you and then  what effect it's having on your mom and  now you have a daughter right  in 2014 I got the honor to open the crow  Metro at New York Fashion Week which was  something I was told many times would  never even be possible but to get to  open that one and then walk the Serena  Williams show and closed that one was  such a huge milestone for our industry  and our possibilities and opportunities  and I think that was the first moment  that people really saw us and we're like  oh my god  why wouldn't they walk alongside  alongside regular models why would we  exclude them we should just have a  lineup of fully diverse individual women  who can rock the clothes because that's  the goal is for us to be able to look at  the runways to look at the billboards to  look at these ads and see ourselves and  know that we can rock that cool clothing  as well but there is pushback guys and I  know you've heard it from people say but  you're promoting unhealthy life you're  you're promoting unhealthy body types  you're promoting obesity that this is  not a good thing to promote and to that  you say what well we we definitely hear  that feedback from people and to me that  really underscores a bias that we have  in our society you know Beyonce said  perfection is a disease of a nation it's  the soul that needs a surgery and I  think that's really true that bias is  really held very deep in our country and  what's happening is by really focusing  on this standard of beauty that promotes  thinness we leave so many women out and  we really perpetuate shame and I don't  think that shame is a motivator for  healthy behavior I think we're talking  about health but what about mental  health and this self-love that you need  to be confident and exist in the world  and heal yourself right I think the  moment that women can see themselves in  the media and in these features  alongside everyone and we have these  diverse campaigns what we create is this  internal happiness that starts to seep  out and I think when we create this  generation of women who are supporting  each other and who are happy we change  the playing field eliminates bias and we  start casting them in the leading role  right yeah that support exactly I like  that word that phrase Denise internal  happiness  bit of that single-dose yeah thank you  both for coming today

